<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The First Realm Arc ~ founded, fed, gathered, joined

The founding runbook carries the ''command sequence'' for standing one vessel (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook">>). This carries the ''arc'' — what happens across several vessels, several operators, and however long the months take, in an order the model constrains rather than convenience chooses.

''The shape, stated once:'' a node vessel stands as the first seed of the first Cabal-realm of the first Nexus, ''in'' the field called the DreamNet — never founding that field, because a Nexus that founded the DreamNet would stand as the thing that can act alone over everything (`identity-enacts-relation#the-constructor`). The base runs down into the dark and terminates in no founder standing outside the series.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-four-joins>>

!! Four different acts, all called //joining// in plain speech

Ordinary speech fuses these. The model runs them as four distinct acts with four different costs, and conflating any two produces a wrong build:

|! plain speech |! the act |! what it moves |! built? |
| //my phone joins// | a leaf ''CONTRACTED'' into a standing fleet | one delegation edge, signed elsewhere; the leaf mints NO root | named, never walked |
| //another operator joins the mesh// | a ''carriage contract'' — wax-sealed, kahu-quorum counter-signed | who relays whose sealed bytes; carry ⊥ read | BUILT (`nexus accept-carriage` · `nexus admit`) |
| //someone joins the realm// | ''DWELLING'' — the first `hoʻokipa`, and the `nohopapa` it deposits | standing in a realm; nothing on the carriage plane | the machinery stands (`feedCabalRealm`); the verb does not |
| //a stranger joins// | a crossing priced at the lineage gate, or a boot-invite at the anon floor | reach, never membership; refusal leaves the party at the floor | BUILT (`admitOnLineage` · `boot-invite`) |

''The one that matters most reads THIRD.'' Carriage and dwelling run on ''orthogonal axes'' — a contracted operator carries sealed traffic and may dwell in no realm at all; a dweller deposits `papa` and may carry nothing. The runbook's ⊕ Grow covers carriage and says nothing about dwelling, which reads as the largest gap in the rite rather than an omission in its prose.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-arc>>

!! The arc, in order

''① The vessel stands.'' The runbook's ⓪–⑥, rehearsed first in containers until it bores, then once on the keeper. Three founding PersonaGroups seat the charter at 2-of-3.

> ''Name what the founding actually produces, so nobody later reads it as more.'' Three PersonaGroups held by ONE human means one locus of cost at both ends of every hold. The gauge reads that realm ''nominal'' — Ψ ≈ 0, no non-aggregativity, a realm that can act alone against what it holds — and reads it correctly. That costs nothing, because ''the gauge lifts and the lift EXPIRES'': the declaration carries the floor, depth accrues later, and an unrenewed standing lapses without anyone striking it. A founding that gauged deep on day one would name a broken instrument.
>
> And the quorum: three keys on one machine make a quorum ''cryptographically real and not real as a check'' — no independent hand can refuse. Record it as the honest founding state, so a later reader never mistakes a seated quorum for a distributed one.

''② The realm gets FED.'' A realm mints no DID and hosts on no machine; nothing stands to create. It begins when someone feeds it, and it would cease if feeding stopped — //there is no commons without commoning// (`cabal-realm#verb-not-noun`). ''The first offering IS the founding of the realm'', never a step after it. The vessel is founded; the Cabal is fed.

''③ The fleet gathers.'' The operator's own device-vessels — a laptop browser, a phone, a tablet — join the standing fleet as ''CONTRACTED'' leaves: each mints its own vessel key on-device, and the persona root signs a delegation EDGE into it from the vessel that already holds the root. ''BIND BY EDGE, NEVER BY SEED'' — a key copied between vessels //is// the collector.

> These add ''no locus of cost''. Below the phase change, holds bind one principal's instruments; the gauge still reads the realm nominal, and correctly. A fleet buys reach, never depth.

''④ A second operator founds their own.'' Same rite, their own hearth, their own PersonaGroups, their own charter. ''Independent genesis'' — no shared root, no descent from the first Nexus, and rejoining the mesh later reads as ''RECOGNITION of a foreign chain'', never reconciliation of a forked one (`identity-enacts-relation`).

''⑤ They dwell in the first realm.'' Here the arc reaches what the code does not: a second operator ''dwelling in'' the ADC realm rather than merely carrying for it. Two distinct loci of cost holding each other — the first genuine `hoʻokipa` in the system, the first hold the gauge can read as non-aggregative, and the first `papa` deposited by anyone but the founder.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-the-runbook-lacks>>

!! A realm and a Nexus name different things, and neither contains the other

''A CABAL REALM'' names //a set of CRDT documents and CID/CAD pointers a cabal of nakama-friends share
amongst each other//. Its members hold it because they hold each other; its reach ends where that
company ends.

''A NEXUS'' names //all operators and users on a magically-federated mesh//. More than one may stand,
public or private, because entering one asks for a ''contract'' and for ''grammar agreement'' — a mesh
admits by consent and by a shared way of reading, never by proximity.

''THE KAHU CABAL'' names neither: it stands as the ''group-seed of the Nexus'', the seat from which a
community manages the mesh IT-as-a-community keeps. A cabal of friends sharing documents and a cabal
of stewards seeding a mesh wear one word and do different work — ''read which one a sentence means
before carrying a rule across''.

The scope leaves follow the same cut. A ''circle-scope'' leaf answers to a compartment of friends; a
''nexus-scope'' leaf answers to the island a charter names. One MAC domain across both would let the
company a human keeps collide with the mesh they stand on.

!! What a maintenance roll must carry, wherever the ledger sits

A realm's standing rides ''per-writer lease slots'' — one slot per face, the effective epoch the MAX
over all of them. That shape is why a low roll costs nothing and a high one converges without a
coordinator, and it decides what a roll must prove.

''A roll binds REALM, WRITER and EPOCH together.'' Each alone is liftable: a roll carried to another
realm would offer maintenance a face never made, and a roll lifted to a higher number could never be
undone — ''the max never decreases, so a forged high roll is IRREVERSIBLE''. The property that makes
the lease safe under no-global-now is exactly what makes forgery unfixable.

Where the slots sit under a vessel's own bag the seal asks nothing the bag did not already answer:
one hand owns the bag. It becomes load-bearing wherever the ledger is ''shared'' — a doc every dweller
may write, where a slot uri is guessable and the seal is the only thing tying a roll to its writer. An
unverifiable slot reads IGNORED: never folded, never fatal.

!! Three stretches the rite does not walk

The runbook stands ''Synthesis-Canon and complete for what it covers''. These sit outside it rather than wrong within it:

# ''The fleet join'' (arc ③). Movement ② names the CONTRACTED founding mode and walks only the SELF-STOOD path. A leaf admitted into a standing fleet mints no root and carries only the edge — the ruling stands (`vessel-standing`), the commands stay unwritten, and a browser vessel differs from a node vessel by device-gated caps rather than by class.
# ''The realm feeding'' (arc ②). No movement names the first offering. `feedCabalRealm` stands built and unwalked, and the rite goes LIVE without the realm it was founded as a seed of ever beginning.
# ''The dwelling join'' (arc ⑤). ⊕ Grow contracts carriage and welcomes users at the anon floor. Neither act seats a dweller. This one wants ''design before commands'' — the conversion act stays UN-MODELLED by ruling (`the-thing-event#unmodelled`), so what the mesh may hold is ''that a re-anchoring happened'', never what made it valid.

''And one naming law belongs here before anyone names the first realm.'' `cabal` names the SCALE, never a required word in anyone's name. This realm carries the word by its founders' choice; a Siletz realm, an ʻohana realm, a Blacksky realm name themselves in their own tongue and lose nothing structural by it.

